.SH Name
ansible_selinux \- Security Enhanced Linux Policy for Ansible

.SH Description
.PP
Security-Enhanced Linux (SELinux) secures Ansible actions via flexible
mandatory access control (MAC).
.PP
For Linux system administrators, Ansible is a very powerful tool to manage and
automate the deployment and configuration of their managed hosts.
.PP
Yet, "with great power comes great responsibility"... So, system administrators
must be careful when using Ansible to manage their machines and make sure that
Ansible is not misused by malicious users to compromise their managed hosts nor
to exfiltrate sensitive information from them.
.PP
To help them secure their managed hosts, implement the information security
policies of their organization, apply the principle of least privilege and
reduce the surface of attack of their managed hosts, the \fBLHQG\fR company has
developed an open source SELinux policy module to confine the Ansible processes
on the managed hosts.
.PP
The basic idea is to confine the Ansible processes in a dedicated SELinux domain
and to define a set of SELinux rules to restrict the access of these processes
to the managed host resources (files, directories, network ports, other
processes...).
.PP
When properly configured, the Ansible processes should execute with the
\fIansible_t\fR SELinux type (domain).
.PP
You can check if you have these processes running by executing the \fBps\fR
command with the \fB\-Z\fR qualifier.
.PP
For example:
.RS 2
\fBps \-eZ | grep ansible_t\fR
.RE

.SH Entrypoints
.PP
Since Ansible is a framework and not a single binary, there is no binary entry
point to the ansible_t SELinux type/domain.
.RE

.SH Linux user (SELinux login) mapping to SELinux user
.PP
The selinux_ansible policy module defines the \fBansible_u\fR SELinux user and
the \fBansible_r\fR SELinux role.
.PP
For Ansible processes to be effectively confined in the
\fBansible_u:ansible_r:ansible_t\fR
SELinux context when the Ansible runner connects to the managed host, system
administrators must map the Linux account used by the Ansible runner
host/platform uses.
.PP
For instance, if the Ansible runner connects to the managed host with the
\fIansible\fR account, this account must be mapped to the \fBansible_u\fR
SELinux user.
.PP
.SS "SELinux login global mapping"
.PP
The Linux account used by Ansible can be mapped regardless of the method (PAM
service) it connects to the managed host.
.PP
Such SELinux login mapping are managed using the \fBsemanage login\fR command.
.PP
For example:
.RS 2
\fBsemanage login --add -s ansible_u -r s0 ansible\fR
.RE
.SS "SELinux login method/service specific mapping"
.PP
The Linux account used by Ansible can be mapped for a specific method (PAM
service) it connects to the managed host with.
.PP
Such SELinux login mapping are managed deploying a text file in the
\fB/etc/selinux/targeted/login\fR directory.
.br
The file name must be the Linux account name used by Ansible to connect to the
managed host.
.br
This file contains one line per specific method/service to map the Linux
account. Each line must comply with the following format:
.RS 2
\fB<SELinux login>:<SELinux user>:<SELinux level range>\fR
.RE
.PP
For example:
.RS 2
File \fB/etc/selinux/targeted/logins/ansible\fR:
.RS 4
\fBsshd:ansible_u:s0\fR
.RE

.SH Process types
.PP
SELinux defines process types (domains) for each process running on the system.
Policy governs the access confined processes have to files/directories and all
other types of resources on the system (network ports, other processes...). 
.PP
.SS "Ansible process type: ansible_t"
.PP
The \fBansible_t\fR process type (domain) is defined for Ansible processes.
.br
This process type is used to confine the Ansible processes and restrict their
access to files, directories and other resources on the managed host.
.PP
The \fBansible_t\fR process type is designed to behave as a confined process
type with low privileges on the managed host. Its scope of action is limited
so that it cannot fully manage the host and its resources.
.br
For instance, the \fBansible_t\fR process type is not allowed to manage
(stop/start/restart...) systemd services nor can it reboot the managed host.
.PP
.SS "Privilege escalation, Ansible process type: Bansible_sudo_t"
.PP
The \fBansible_sudo_t\fR process type (domain) is defined for Ansible processes
when privilege escalation is used using the \fBsudo\fR command as the Asnible
\fBbecome\fR mechanism.
.PP
The \fBansible_sudo_t\fR process type is designed to behave as a confined
process type with high privileges on the managed host.
.br
Its scope of action is extended in terms of SELinux policy rules,
so that it can (almost) fully manage the host and its resources.
.br
Yet, its effective privileges are limited by the \fBsudo\fR command
configuration and the \fBsudoers\fR configuration file.
.PP
.SS "Permissive ansible_t process type - \fINOT RECOMMENDED\fR"
.PP
When the SELinux policy is too restrictive for the Ansible processes to perform
all their legitimate tasks, in last resort the system administrator can decide
to make the \fBansible_t\fR and/or \fBansible_sudo_t\fR process type(s)
\fIpermissive\fR.
.PP
Making the ansible_t process type permissive:
.RS 2
\fBsemanage permissive \-a ansible_t\fR
.RE
.PP
\fBBEWARE: Permissive process types are not denied any access by SELinux.\fR
.br
Access Vector Cache (AVC) messages will still be generated for each access
attempt.
.RE
.PP

.SH Booleans
.PP
The SELinux policy rules for the ansible_t domain can be tuned using predefined
booleans to allow/disallow the Ansible runner different actions on the managed
host.
.PP
\fBallow_ansible_connectto_http\fR (true)
.RS 4
Whether to allow the Ansible processes to connect (TCP) to HTTP ports (labeled
as http_port_t).
.RE
.PP
\fBallow_ansible_connectto_ldap\fR (true)
.RS 4
Whether to allow the Ansible processes to connect (TCP) to LDAP ports (labeled
as ldap_port_t).
.RE
.PP
\fBallow_ansible_manage_services\fR (true)
.RS 4
Whether to allow the Ansible processes to \fBfully\fR manage \fBany\fR systemd
service unit.
.br
By default, the ansible_t domain is only allowed to query systemd for unit
statuses.
.RE
.PP
\fBallow_sysadm_read_ansible_files\fR (true)
.RS 4
Whether to allow the \fBsysadm_t\fR SELinux domain to read Ansible files (logs,
cache...) on the managed hosts.
.RE

.SH File Contexts
.PP
\fBansible_conf_t\fR
.RS 4
Files containing (not highly sensitive) local Ansible configuration properties
and information. (Cannot be altered by the Ansible processes)
.RE
.PP
\fBansible_log_t\fR
.RS 4
Ansible execution local log files, may contain sensitive information.
(Append-only access by the Ansible processes, by default)
.RE
.PP
\fBansible_cache_t\fR
.RS 4
Ansible (various) ache files, persistent across Ansible connections.
.RE
.PP
\fBansible_tmp_t\fR
.RS 4
Ansible temporary files.
.RE
.PP
\fBansible_bin_t\fR
.RS 4
Ansible specific binaries which Ansible processes are allowed to execute but
which other confined processes cannot.
(Cannot be altered by the Ansible processes)
.RE
.PP
\fBansible_lib_t\fR
.RS 4
Ansible libraries files which Ansible processes are allowed to execute but
which other confined processes cannot.
(Cannot be altered by the Ansible application)
.RE

.SH Interfaces
.PP
The Ansible SELinux policy is shipped with a set of "interfaces" to easily
extend the policy.
.br
To use an given interface a small SELinux policy module source code must be
created, compiled and finally loaded.
.RE

.SH Author
.PP
The Ansible SELinux policy was initially authored by Hubert Quarantel-Colombani
and is now published and maintained by LHQG <https://lhqg.fr/>
.RE

